# Nothing is set here, and that is the point.
#
# This file used to open by saying that an undeclared dependency must not
# resolve, which is true and was not what the three settings under it did. They
# were `strict-peer-dependencies`, `auto-install-peers` and `resolution-mode`:
# all three about peers and version choice, two of them relaxations, and each
# one reproducing what pnpm 10 already does. Removing them leaves the lockfile
# byte for byte and the install exit code unchanged, which is how that was
# settled rather than argued.
#
# What actually stops a package importing what it never declared is pnpm's
# isolated `node_modules`, which is its default and needs nothing written down.
# `tooling/boundaries.test.ts` measures it: an import of a sibling that is not a
# dependency fails as unresolvable, before any architecture rule is consulted.
#
# They also cost three warnings from npm on every `npx`, since npm reads this
# file and knows none of those keys.
#
# So: if something belongs here, it needs a reason of its own, and the reason
# goes beside it.
